{{Politics of Ukraine}}

Presidential elections were held in [[Ukraine]] on 17 January 2010. As no candidate received a majority of the vote, a run-off election was held between Prime Minister [[Yulia Tymoshenko]] and opposition leader [[Viktor Yanukovych]] on 7 February.

On 14 February, Yanukovych was declared President-elect and winner with 50% of the popular vote. According to [[s:Constitution of Ukraine#Article 104|Article 104 of Ukraine's Constitution]], the president had to be sworn into office within 30 days of the official declaration of the results.<ref name=cec-declaration>[http://www.cvk.gov.ua/news/protokol_cvk_07022010.pdf CEC official declaration of the 2010 Presidential election]{{Dead link|date=July 2018 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=no }}, [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]]</ref> Parliament subsequently scheduled Yanukovych's inauguration for 25 February.<ref name=inauguration>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59703/ Update: Yanukovych to be sworn in, rival fights on] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120612030958/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59703/ |date=12 June 2012 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (14 February 2010)</ref>

On 17 February, the Supreme Administrative Court of Ukraine suspended the results following an appeal by Tymoshenko. The court suspended the [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission]]'s ruling that had declared Yanukovych as the winner of the election, but did not postpone or cancel his inauguration.<ref>https://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/18/world/europe/18ukraine.html {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141110122544/http://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/18/world/europe/18ukraine.html |date=10 November 2014 }} High Court in Ukraine Weighs Appeal on Election</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5hYHMIGxCNY8KpokmVs53ufhC8qIQD9DTT0H00 |title=Ukrainian election results suspended on appeal |access-date=11 December 2018 |archive-date=21 February 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100221065939/https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5hYHMIGxCNY8KpokmVs53ufhC8qIQD9DTT0H00 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8519922.stm |title=Ukrainian election result suspended after PM's appeal |date=17 February 2010 |access-date=17 February 2010 |archive-date=28 February 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170228052622/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8519922.stm |url-status=live }}</ref> On 20 February, Tymoshenko withdrew her appeal.<ref name=NYTPMD>[https://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/21/world/europe/21ukraine.html Ukraine Prime Minister Drops Election Challenge] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220528/https://www.nytimes.com/2010/02/21/world/europe/21ukraine.html |date=16 January 2023 }}, ''[[The New York Times]]'' (20 February 2010)</ref>{{clarify|reason=Who won the election, who was inaugurated president, when, and for how long a term?|date=May 2022}}

==Background==
{{expand section|reason=The 2004 "[[Orange revolution]]" should be mentioned in the context of these elections|date=November 2016}}
In Ukraine, the previous two presidential first-round ballots had traditionally occurred in October.

According to the [[s:Constitution of Ukraine#Article 103|Constitution of Ukraine]], regular elections of the president of Ukraine are held on the last Sunday of the last month of the fifth year of the term of authority of the president of Ukraine. In the event of pre-term termination of authority of the president of Ukraine, elections of the president of Ukraine are held within ninety days from the day of the president's office falling vacant.

Early presidential elections can be held in case of a president's resignation, ill-health, [[impeachment]] or death.

The president of Ukraine is elected by the citizens of Ukraine for a five-year term, on the basis of universal, equal and direct suffrage, by secret ballot.

A candidate seeking election must be a citizen of Ukraine who has attained the age of thirty-five, has the right to vote, has resided in Ukraine for the past ten years prior to the day of elections, and has command of the state language as required by [[s:Constitution of Ukraine#Article 103|Article 103 of Ukraine's Constitution]].

On 1 April 2009, the [[Verkhovna Rada]] designated 25 October 2009, as the date for the first round of voting. Within a week, President Yushchenko filed an appeal with the Constitutional Court against Verkhovna Rada's 25 October date. The President's appeal argued that his inauguration on 23 January 2005, was the commencement of his five-year term of office and as such the next presidential election must be set for the last Sunday before 23 January 2010, in accordance with Article 103.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-310155.html Yushchenko appeals parliament's decision to call presidential election for Oct. 25] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220541/https://www.unian.info/politics/209287-yushchenko-appeals-parliaments-decision-to-call-presidential-election-for-oct-25.html |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (8 April 2009)</ref>

On 13 May 2009, the court ruled in Yushchenko's favor, striking out the 25 October date for the elections.<ref>[http://www.pr-inside.com/ukraine-court-rules-election-date-unconstitutional-r1251445.htm Ukraine court rules election date unconstitutional] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120328190927/http://www.pr-inside.com/ukraine-court-rules-election-date-unconstitutional-r1251445.htm |date=28 March 2012 }}, PR-inside.com (13 May 2009)</ref> On 14 May 2009, the [[Party of Regions]] leader [[Viktor Yanukovych]] stated that the presidential elections should now be held on 17 January 2010.<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/13852/ Yanukovych agrees with Yushchenko on presidential election date], [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (14 May 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/13747/ Court declares unconstitutional parliament's resolution calling presidential polls for October 25, 2009], [[Interfax|Interfax-Ukraine]] (13 May 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-315428.html Constitutional Court rules against presidential poll date] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220547/https://www.unian.info/politics/220618-constitutional-court-rules-against-presidential-poll-date.html |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (13 May 2009)</ref>

On 23 June 2009, the [[Verkhovna Rada|Parliament]] rescheduled the date for the election for Sunday, 17 January 2010, with 399 lawmakers out of 442 lawmakers registered in the session hall voted "for" the resolution "On appointing of regular election of President of Ukraine".

[[Ministry of Internal Affairs (Ukraine)|Minister of Internal Affairs]] [[Yuriy Lutsenko]] said on 21 September 2009 that he believes that the lists of voters at this presidential election will be more qualitative and more "clear" than it was at [[Ukrainian Presidential election|previous elections]] because "double names" were removed from the list.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-337162.html All doubles are eliminated – Lutsenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220547/https://www.unian.info/politics/267725-all-doubles-are-eliminated-lutsenko.html |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (21 September 2009)</ref> The same day the [[Party of Regions]] complained about a lot of mistakes in that list and that the number of voters fell in the Southern Ukraine and Eastern Ukraine and increased by 0.5–1% in Western Ukraine.<ref name=List>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/20606/ Regions Party worried about numerous inaccuracies in state register of voters], [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (21 September 2009)</ref> It is the first time the state register of voters will be used in a Ukrainian election.<ref name=List/>

==Electoral system==
Ukraine's president is elected by a [[two-round system|two-round]] [[first-past-the-post]] voting system. The first round of voting was held on 17 January 2010. As no candidate in the first-round ballot had 50% or more votes the two highest polling candidates faced off in a second-round ballot<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57219/ Presidential election gets under way in Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120603125154/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57219/ |date=3 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (17 January 2010)</ref> which was held on 7 February 2010. Viktor Yanukovych received the highest vote (48.96%) and was expected to be declared the winner.<ref name="Law_President_Election">{{cite web|url=http://www.cvk.gov.ua/metod/kultura/npa/vp_26102009.htm|title=Law on the election of the President of Ukraine article 85 (16)|work=[[Ukrainian legislature]]|publisher=[[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]]|access-date=2009-08-21}}</ref> Under Ukrainian law presidents elect must take the oath within 30 days of the declaration of the poll, which must be made before 17 February 2010.

On 24 July 2009, the [[Verkhovna Rada]] (Ukrainian parliament) amended the law on presidential elections reducing the official presidential campaign from 120 to 90 days.<ref>[http://www.cvk.gov.ua/metod/kultura/npa/vp.htm Law on Presidential Elections], [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]] (Ukrainian)</ref><ref>[http://www.venice.coe.int/docs/2009/CDL-EL%282009%29023-e.asp Law on Presidential Elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091014235248/http://www.venice.coe.int/docs/2009/CDL-EL(2009)023-e.asp |date=2009-10-14 }}, [[Venice Commission]]</ref>
<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-327957.html VR reduced term of presidential campaign from 120 to 90&nbsp;days] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220553/https://gum.criteo.com/syncframe?origin=publishertag&topUrl=www.unian.info |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (24 July 2009)</ref> Outgoing President Viktor Yushchenko refused to sign the new law and lodged an appeal in Ukraine's Constitutional Court, but failed to outline in detail the grounds for any appeal.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/47329 Law on presidential elections will be amended during election campaign, says Yuschenko's secretariat], [[Kyiv Post]] (21 August 2009)</ref> The speaker of the parliament, Volodymyr Lytvyn, signed the amended law into existence following the President's refusal to sign it, despite this being illegal.<ref name="howVRmakeslawsinDec2012">[http://rada.gov.ua/en/news/News/News/71072.html The interns of the Program of Internship at the Verkhovna Rada of Ukraine and Central Executive Bodies for 2012-2013 learned the procedure of submission and passage of bills in the Verkhovna Rada] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130928133814/http://rada.gov.ua/en/news/News/News/71072.html |date=28 September 2013 }}, [[Verkhovna Rada]] (14 December 2012)<br />[https://books.google.com/books?id=p0aPIjwXDioC&dq=Verkhovna+Rada+%22three+readings%22&pg=PT129 Ukraine: Energy Policy Review 2006], [[International Energy Agency]], 24 October 2006, {{ISBN|9264109919}} (page 130)</ref>

Maryna Stavniychuk, deputy head of the presidential secretariat and the President's spokesperson on legal matters, stated ''"It is obvious that there are no serious political or legal grounds to consider the issue of the possible disruption of the presidential elections in Ukraine."'' <ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/49470 Ukraine's presidential elections will not be disrupted], [[Kyiv Post]] (25 September 2009)</ref>

The amended law on the presidential elections required candidates to pay a [[Ukrainian hryvnia|₴]]2,500,000 (~308,000 [[United States dollar|USD]]) nomination deposit which will only be refunded to the two highest polling candidates that progressed to the second round of voting.

On 19 October 2009, the [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]] formed the 225 territorial election districts needed for carrying out the election.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-342037.html CEC formed 225 territorial election districts] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220553/https://gum.criteo.com/syncframe?origin=publishertag&topUrl=www.unian.info |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (19 October 2009)</ref>

20 October 2009, Ukraine's Constitutional Court announced its ruling declaring unconstitutional five aspects of the new law of the presidential election. Voters abroad will no longer have to be registered with the Ukrainian consulate in order to cast a vote. The courts will retain the right to consider without limitations any application or appeal in respect to a candidate's registration or the conduct of the election. The cancellation of absentee ballots remains as does the 90-day election period and the ₴2.5&nbsp;million deposit. The ruling of the Constitutional Court is not expected to impact seriously on the election as the amended legislation remains in place.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/ukr/news/news-342226.html Constitutional court rules on Law of President elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220551/https://www.unian.ua/politics/278736-ks-zarubav-novi-pravila-gri-na-prezidentskih-viborah.html |date=16 January 2023 }}, [[UNIAN]] (20 October 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.ccu.gov.ua/doccatalog/document?id=75929 Ruling 26/2009 Compliance with the Constitution of Ukraine (constitutionality) of certain provisions of laws of Ukraine "On elections of President of Ukraine" On State Register of Voters, "" On Amending Certain Legislative Acts of Ukraine on the presidential elections in Ukraine and the Code of Administrative Procedure Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110728172536/http://www.ccu.gov.ua/doccatalog/document?id=75929 |date=2011-07-28 }}, [[Constitutional Court of Ukraine]] (19 October 2009)</ref>

On 21 December 2009, the Central Election Commission of Ukraine formed 113 foreign polling stations.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55701/ Ukraine's CEC forms 113 foreign polling stations for presidential elections], [[Kyiv Post]] (22 December 2009)</ref>

Voters are permitted to vote at home during the presidential election.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56445/ Tymoshenko to appeal against CEC decision permitting home voting during presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100108123132/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56445/ |date=8 January 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (5 January 2010)</ref>

===Costs===
The [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission]] has estimated the budget of the holding of regular presidential elections in Ukraine at ₴1.5&nbsp;billion,<ref>[http://en.for-ua.com/news/2009/07/29/102946.html Presidential direct election to cost Ukraine over 1.5&nbsp;billion UAH] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711020241/http://en.for-ua.com/news/2009/07/29/102946.html |date=2011-07-11 }}, For-ua (29 July 2009)</ref> (approximately 200&nbsp;million [[United States dollar|US dollars]]) with additional costs required by candidates to fund their campaigns.

On 26 November, the Central Election Commission stated a total of ₴1.314&nbsp;billion is required to hold the presidential election, including 192.2&nbsp;million in 2009 and 1.122&nbsp;billion in 2010.<ref name=numberc>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53669/ Central Election Commission cuts expenditure on presidential election by Hr 17.3&nbsp;million] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120606143202/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53669/ |date=6 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (26 November 2009)</ref>

Assessments by political analysts show that each presidential candidate will have to spend at least US$150–200mn to promote himself; this includes buying story lines in the media, visual advertising, canvassing, printing political material and, work with electoral commissions.<ref>[https://web.archive.org/web/20090915223028/http://en.proua.com/pulse%20of%20the%20week/2009/08/26/181226.html How much for today's president?], proUa (26 August 2009)</ref>

Chairman of the Committee of Voters of Ukraine, Oleksandr Chernenko, also commented that presidential candidates will spend 1&nbsp;billion US dollars on the election campaign<ref>[http://en.for-ua.com/news/2009/10/20/175926.html Presidential candidates will spend at least USD 1&nbsp;billion on election campaign in Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711020253/http://en.for-ua.com/news/2009/10/20/175926.html |date=2011-07-11 }}, ForUm (20 October 2009)</ref>

The cost of the run-off ballot is estimated to be US$119&nbsp;million<ref>[http://www.ukrinform.ua/eng/order/?id=179639 Run-off to take UAH 448.581, 119&nbsp;million – CEC ] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120225015254/http://www.ukrinform.ua/eng/order/?id=179639 |date=2012-02-25 }}, UkrInform (28 January 2010)</ref>

===Timetable===
Ukraine's Central Electoral Commission (CEC) has set the following timetable for the conduct of the election:<ref name=timetable/><ref name=CECtimetable>{{cite web |url=http://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/acts/getd?id=24265&ptext= |title=Official text (Ukrainian), CEC |access-date=2009-10-03 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110607094440/http://www.cvk.gov.ua/pls/acts/getd?id=24265&ptext= |archive-date=2011-06-07 |url-status=dead }}</ref>
* 19 October: Official 90-day Election Campaign period commences
* 20 October to 6 November: Nominations open
* 9 November: Deadline for nomination document/submissions
<blockquote>CEC has five days to assess and approve or reject nominations</blockquote>
* 11 November: Deadline for candidates to submit any corrections to documentation
* 13 November: CEC to finalize registration of nominations
* 15 November: CEC to publish nomination lists of candidates
<blockquote>CEC provides certified copies of the voters list to all candidates. Within Three days of registration Candidates must provide a statement of assets and income. Candidates allowed to commence official campaign one day after registration is finalized.</blockquote>
* 9 December: Foreign polling stations to be set up.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/53281/ Foreign polling stations for Ukrainian presidential elections to be set up by Dec. 9], [[Kyiv Post]] (20 November 2009)</ref>
* 15 December: CEC to approve ballot paper format ready for printing.
* 21 December: Deadline for withdrawals of candidature.
* 2 January: Last day for public opinion polls to be published prior to election.
* 9 January: All ballot papers to be printed and ready for distribution to polling stations/districts.
* 15 January: Last day of public campaigning before polling day
* 17 January: Election (first-round ballot) polling commences 8&nbsp;am and closes 10&nbsp;pm
* 22 January: Tabulation of overseas and territorial polling place to be completed
* 27 January: Determination of voting results and declaration of poll

As no single candidate had 50% or more of the total recorded vote the two highest polling candidates faced off in a second-round ballot. The candidate with the highest vote in the second round will win the election.
* 7 February: Final round run-off ballot
* 17 February: Declaration of the election must be made within 10&nbsp;days following the ballot or within 3&nbsp;days of receipt of the official election protocols.

According to Article 104 of Ukraine's Constitution the President-elect within 30&nbsp;days of the declaration of the poll must be sworn into office before Ukraine's parliament
* 25 February: Viktor Yanukovych is sworn into office.

