===Excluded candidates===
All together the Central Election Commission had rejected sixteen applications for candidate registration.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/51773/ 16 applications have been rejected for registration as presidential candidates] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091106202644/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/51773/ |date=6 November 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (13 November 2009)</ref>

The Central Election Commission refused to register Oleksandr Hordiichuk, Olena Osnach, Oleksandr Luzan, Hanna Kostiv, Oleksandr Vaschenko, Oleksandr Ohorodnikov, Vasyl Handula, Yurii Petlevana, Petro Rekalo, Anatolii Polischuk, Mykhailo Hamaniuk, Oleksandr Vretyk, Artem Polezhaka, Oleh Omelchenko, [[Natalia Vitrenko]],<ref name=2more/> [[Mykola Melnychenko]], Serhii Martyian and Serhiy Schetinin. The reason stated was due to errors in their documentation, qualifications or failure to pay the required ₴2.5&nbsp;million nomination deposit.<ref>[http://www.ukranews.com/eng/article/228455.html CEC Refuses To Register Kulychenko, Subbotin, Polyschuk, Honcharenko, Melnyk As Presidential Candidates]{{Dead link|date=April 2019|bot=InternetArchiveBot|fix-attempted=yes}}, [[Ukrainian News Agency]] (30 October 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/51773/ CEC denies registration to four more contenders for Ukraine's presidency] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091106202644/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/51773/ |date=6 November 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (3 November 2009)</ref><ref>{{in lang|ru}} [http://www.vitrenko.org/start.php?lang=1&part_id=1&article_id=9088 Украина обречена либо на распад, либо на революцию. Для украинской власти Конституция Украины – туалетная бумажка. Заявление Лидера ПСПУ Наталии Витренко] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190916170436/http://www.vitrenko.org/start.php?lang=1&part_id=1&article_id=9088 |date=2019-09-16 }}, Official website of [[Natalia Vitrenko]] (11 November 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/24591/ Eighteen to run for Ukraine's presidency] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120329012131/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/24591/ |date=29 March 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (12 November 2009)</ref>

Nominations by [[Political parties in Ukraine|parties]] and candidates to run in the election closed on 6 November 2009.<ref name=timetable>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/49902 Ukraine's presidential candidates to be nominated from Oct. 20 to Nov. 6] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091005020722/http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/49902 |date=5 October 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (2 October 2009)</ref> Eighteen candidates in all have been nominated. The Central Election Committee had until 11 November 2009 to process documentation and finalize the election list.

==Campaign==
<gallery perrow="5">
File:Yatsenyuk tent 2009.jpg|Yatsenyuk promotion (August 2009)
File:Реклама Тимошенко 2010 рік.jpg|"She Works" billboard (August 2009)
File:Crowd before start 25dec09 3078.JPG|''Нет Ворюле!'', anti-Tymoshenko placard,<br />rally Dnipropetrovsk, 25 Dec 2009
File:2Normal 25Dec09 3288.JPG|Concert and rally for Yanukovych,<br />Dnipropetrovsk, 25 Dec 2009
</gallery>
The official presidential campaign commenced on 19 October 2009, with nominations opening on 20 October through to 6 November. The "unofficial" campaign had already started during the summer of 2009 with tents of [[Yatsenyuk's Front for Change|Front for Change]] distributing campaign material for [[Arseniy Yatsenyuk]] Front for Changes and large scale and billboards stating ''Others make problems. She Works'' (in the colors and letter type of [[Bloc Yulia Tymoshenko]]), and photos of [[Sergei Tigipko]] displayed in most Ukrainian towns and TV-adds of [[Yulia Tymoshenko]] and [[Volodymyr Lytvyn]] shown on national TV.<ref>{{in lang|uk}} [http://www3.pravda.com.ua/news/2009/9/3/100839.htm Від анти-кризи до армійських наметів та "фашистської загрози"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090906063109/http://www.pravda.com.ua/news/2009/9/3/100839.htm |date=2009-09-06 }}, [[Ukrainska Pravda]] (3 September 2009)</ref><ref name=EDM>[http://georgiandaily.com/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=14967&Itemid=132 Eurasia Daily Monitor, The Jamestown Foundation—October 1, 2009—Volume 6, Issue 180] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110711091033/http://georgiandaily.com/index.php?option=com_content&task=view&id=14967&Itemid=132 |date=11 July 2011 }}, [[The Jamestown Foundation]] (1 October 2009)</ref> According to Tymoshenko the "She Works" billboards were paid for by the [[Fatherland Party (Ukraine)|Fatherland Party]], and therefore they were also "social". [[Party of Regions]] deputy Andry Paruby officially requested that the prosecutor-general's office investigates the sources of financing of Tymoshenko's advertisements. He suggested that public money might have been used.<ref name=EDM/>

Ukraine has proven more than once the degree to which the success of an election campaign depends on the level of professionalism and political spin techniques applied in election campaigns.<ref>[http://en.proua.com/pulse%20of%20the%20week/2009/09/02/182426.html See how they run] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090914101051/http://en.proua.com/pulse%20of%20the%20week/2009/09/02/182426.html|date=2009-09-14}}, [[proUA]] (2 September 2009)</ref>

The most popular candidates are former [[Prime Minister of Ukraine|prime minister]] and leader of the [[Party of Regions|Opposition party]] [[Viktor Yanukovych]] and current prime minister [[Yulia Tymoshenko]].

Incumbent [[President (government title)|president]] [[Viktor Yushchenko]]'s support has slumped from a high of 52% in 2004 to below 3% in Ukrainian public opinion polls. Most political commentators regard him as a heavy underdog who stands little chance of being re-elected to a second term of office.<ref>{{cite web|title=Surviving the Crisis in Ukraine (American Progress Forum Video)|url=http://www.americanprogress.org/issues/2009/07/ukraine_event.html|work=[[Center for American Progress]]|date=30 July 2009|access-date=19 August 2009|archive-date=11 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090811030213/http://www.americanprogress.org/issues/2009/07/ukraine_event.html/|url-status=live}}</ref> A recent public opinion poll indicated that 83% of Ukrainians will not vote for Yushchenko.<ref>{{cite news|title=Poll: Ukraine president's chances of re-election slim|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50997|work=[[Kyiv Post]]|date=20 October 2009|access-date=2009-09-20}}</ref>

On 5 April 2009, [[Arseniy Yatseniuk]], former [[chairman of the Verkhovna Rada]], announced his intention to run in the election.<ref>{{cite news|title=Yatsenyuk will be on the ballot for the office of President of Ukraine|url=http://korrespondent.net/ukraine/politics/795471|work=[[Korrespondent.net]]|date=5 April 2009|access-date=2009-04-07|archive-date=7 April 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090407000347/http://korrespondent.net/ukraine/politics/795471|url-status=live}}</ref> His popularity has slowly risen to around 12–14% and is now in third place behind Yanukovych and Tymoshenko.<ref>{{cite news|title=Presidential race: Young candidate so far not candidate of the young|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/46930|work=[[Kyiv Post]]|date=13 August 2009|access-date=2009-08-13|archive-date=19 August 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090819122932/http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/46930|url-status=live}}</ref>

According to [[Oxford Analytica]] the working relationship between President Yushchenko and his Prime Minister Tymoshenko will be further complicated by the search of Yushchenko for partners other than Tymoshenko's [[Bloc Yulia Tymoshenko]] who will ensure his re-election.<ref>[http://www.taraskuzio.net/media18_files/Yushchenko_Tymoshenko.pdf Yushchenko plots his premier's removal] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081121090244/http://www.taraskuzio.net/media18_files/Yushchenko_Tymoshenko.pdf |date=21 November 2008 }}, [[Oxford Analytica]] (11 March 2008)</ref> Since Yushchenko dismissed Tymoshenko as prime minister on 8 September 2005, the relations between Tymoshenko and Yushchenko,<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/7403/ Tymoshenko accuses Yuschenko of obstructing executive authorities' teamwork] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212242/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/7403/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax|Interfax-Ukraine]] (6 February 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/6784/ Yuschenko demands immediate amendments to 2009 budget to save Ukraine's economy – televised address to nation] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212257/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/6784/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax|Interfax-Ukraine]] (30 January 2009)</ref><ref>[http://unian.net/eng/news/news-300192.html Yushchenko, Tymoshenko criticize each other (photo-report)] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090213150513/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-300192.html |date=13 February 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (11 February 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-299984.html Agreement with Russia threatens Ukraine's security – President] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090213194604/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-299984.html |date=13 February 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (10 February 2009)</ref> including the [[Secretariat of the President of Ukraine]],<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/7322/ Presidential secretariat considers PM's report "theatrical performance"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212314/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/7322/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax|Interfax-Ukraine]] (5 February 2009)</ref> have been hostile.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-320314.html Yushchenko calls on Oblast leaders to work out joint plan of actions] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090615154758/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-320314.html |date=15 June 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (10 June 2009)</ref> In an interview with the [[Frankfurter Allgemeine Zeitung]] of 11 February 2009 Tymoshenko said her [[Yulia Tymoshenko#Gas dispute between Russia and Ukraine (2009)|recent conflict with the president]] is a political competition and not [[ideological]] [[wikt:antagonism|antagonisms]] and she emphasized that the "election struggle for the next presidential elections has virtually begun."<ref>[http://www.ukranews.com/eng/article/179917.html Tymoshenko: Political Competition Accounts For Conflict With Yuschenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120315131337/http://www.ukranews.com/eng/article/179917.html |date=2012-03-15 }}, [[Ukrainian News Agency]] (11 February 2009)</ref> During a visit to [[Brussels]] on 10 February 2009, Chairman of the Verkhovna Rada [[Volodymyr Lytvyn]] seconded that.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-300208.html Ukrainian speaker condemns Kyiv's internal bickering] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090213181431/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-300208.html |date=13 February 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]], (11 February 2009)</ref> In late February 2009, President Yushchenko called on all Ukrainian politicians to "stop the presidential election campaign until July 1."<ref>[http://www.ukranews.com/eng/article/183292.html Yuschenko Called On Politicians To Stop Presidential Election Campaign Until July]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, [[Ukrainian News Agency]] (27 February 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/36253 Interview with the president] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090227034726/http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/36253 |date=27 February 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (25 February 2009)</ref>

On 16 June 2009, Tymoshenko accused Yushchenko, Yatseniuk and Yanukovych of having the same campaign headquarters financed by (businessman and) [[RosUkrEnergo]] owner [[Dmytro Firtash]].<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-321211.html Tymoshenko: Yushchenko, Yatseniuk, and Yanukovych have one headquarters for three] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110813153911/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-321211.html |date=13 August 2011 }}, [[UNIAN]] (16 June 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/15621/ Yuschenko and Yatseniuk are 'technical candidates' for Yanukovych, says Tymoshenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212334/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/15621/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (16 June 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.ukranews.com/eng/article/203910.html Tymoshenko Says Yuschenko To Be Yatseniuk's Technical Candidate]{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, [[Ukrainian News Agency]] (16 June 2009)</ref>

Tymoshenko stated on 22 June 2009 that "There is a team work on solving these issues between the President and the Prime Minister. Professional advice and support of the President will help the government during difficult times".<ref>[http://unian.net/eng/news/news-322392.html Tymoshenko says "there is team work" between her and President] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090624041021/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-322392.html |date=24 June 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (22 June 2009)</ref>

On 11 August 2009, [[Russian president]] [[Dmitry Medvedev|Medvedev]] in an open letter<ref>[http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/text/docs/2009/08/220759.shtml Address to the President of Ukraine Victor Yushchenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090815110536/http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/text/docs/2009/08/220759.shtml |date=2009-08-15 }}, [[Kremlin.ru]] (11 August 2009)</ref> directed at Viktor Yushchenko, raised a number of issues of concern related to the perceived "anti-Russian position of the current Ukrainian authorities". The Russian president's comments<ref>[http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/sdocs/vappears.shtml Relations between Russia and Ukraine: a New Era Must Begin] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090430140149/http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/sdocs/vappears.shtml |date=2009-04-30 }}, Video – [[Russian president]] (11 August 2009)</ref> were considered by analysts and others including the president of Ukraine as Russia's interference in Ukraine's domestic affairs.<ref>[https://www.reuters.com/article/companyNewsAndPR/idUSLB59146620090811?pageNumber=1&virtualBrandChannel=0 UPDATE 3-Russia's Medvedev wades into Ukraine polls], [[Reuters]] (August 11, 2009)</ref><ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8195194.stm Medvedev lambasts Ukraine leader], [[BBC News]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18365/ No hope for normalizing relations with Russia under current leadership, says Ukraine's opposition leader] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212414/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18365/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18366/ Medvedev's statement shows Russia wants to impact presidential campaign in Ukraine, says pro-Yuschenko MP] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212440/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18366/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18417/ Yatseniuk says Yuschenko has given grounds to call his policy anti-Russian] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212507/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18417/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18418/ Medvedev's statement may be 'to Yuschenko's advantage,' says Tihipko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212533/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18418/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18419/ Medvedev's message to Yuschenko could be used in election campaign to split Ukraine, says speaker] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212547/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/18419/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 August 2009)</ref>

On 12 September 2009, a tour called "With Ukraine in Heart" in support of Yulia Tymoshenko kick-started on [[Kyiv]]'s [[Maidan Nezalezhnosti]]. The most popular singers and bands of Ukraine took part in the tour.<ref>Artist included [[Ruslana]], [[Oleksandr Ponomariov|Oleksandr Ponomaryov]], [[Ani Lorak]], [[Potap]] and [[Nastia Kamenskikh]], [[Tina Karol]], [[Natalia Mogilevska]], [[Iryna Bilyk]], [[TIK (band)|TIK]], [[TNMK]], "[[Druha Rika]]", [[Mad Heads XL]]. See the concert here [http://www.videoinfo.com.ua/] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090922215248/http://www.videoinfo.com.ua/|date=2009-09-22}}</ref><ref>[http://photo.unian.net/eng/themes/14524 Events by themes: Allukrainian round "With Ukraine in a heart!"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121021202335/http://photo.unian.net/eng/themes/14524 |date=21 October 2012 }}. [[UNIAN]]</ref><ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-335608.html Mogilevska went to Tymoshenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090919025334/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-335608.html |date=19 September 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (11 September 2008)</ref>

On 14 September 2009, the [[Communist Party of Ukraine]], the [[Social Democratic Party of Ukraine (united)]], the [[Justice Party (Ukraine)|Justice Party]] and the [[Union of Leftists]] signed an agreement on creating [[Election bloc of left and central left political forces|the bloc of leftists and center-leftists]] and a unitary participation in the presidential election.<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/20204/ Four parties unite to participate in presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120229130135/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/20204/ |date=2012-02-29 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (14 September 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/21363/ Bloc of left and center-left forces to nominate CPU Leader for Ukraine's president] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120314010307/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/21363/ |date=14 March 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (3 October 2009)</ref>

The Pechersky district court in Kyiv on 22 September 2009, banned "any unfair advertisement" against Tymoshenko in response to a video (allegedly made by the [[Party of Regions]]), which claimed that Tymoshenko does not deliver on her promises. The video reportedly mocked Tymoshenko's main campaign slogan "She Works," which is frequently used in her advertisements.<ref name=EDM/>

In October 2009 representatives of the [[Western Ukrainian]] [[intelligentsia]] called upon the candidates Yushchenko, Yatseniuk, Hrytsenko and "other representatives of national democratic forces" to withdraw in favour of Tymoshenko.<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/21256/ Western Ukrainian intelligentsia calls on candidates for president to withdraw in favor of Tymoshenko] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120314005853/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/21256/ |date=2012-03-14 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (1 October 2009)</ref>

On 6 October 2009, the [[incumbent]] President Yushchenko warned that there may be attempts to use [[Television in Ukraine|regional television]] and [[Radio in Ukraine|radio companies]] to create advantages for the [[Second Tymoshenko Government|government]] in the election campaign.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50174 Vannykova: Yuschenko warns against attempts to monopolize radio and TV broadcasting], [[Kyiv Post]] (6 October 2009)</ref>

On 17 October 2009, The Social-Democratic Party of Ukraine has backed a decision to create the bloc of left and center-left political forces and supported the leader of the Communist Party of Ukraine Petro Symonenko as a single candidate for the post of the Ukrainian president from left political forces<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50859 Social-Democratic Party supports Symonenko as single candidate for president post from left political forces], [[Kyiv Post]] (17 October 2009)</ref>

19 October was the official start of the Elections campaign 90-day period.

20 October candidate nomination registration opens. [[Oleg Riabokon]] first candidate to officially nominate.

On 20 October, Ukrainian Parliament voted to amend Ukraine's Constitution (390 out of 438 in favor) to remove provision related to Parliamentary immunity that prevents a member of parliament from being criminally liable, detained or arrested without the consent of the Verkhovna Rada. An earlier proposal to only remove immunity from the Parliament was defeated. The proposed new provisions also limits presidential immunity. The president can not be detained or arrested without the consent of the parliament; however, on conviction of an offense the president automatically loses office. The proposed amendments have been forwarded to [[Constitutional Court of Ukraine|Ukraine's Constitutional Court]] for review and will need to be reaffirmed by the parliament in February 2010.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/ukr/news/news-342192.html Parliament votes to remove Immunity] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091023050303/http://www.unian.net/ukr/news/news-342192.html |date=23 October 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (20 October 2009)</ref>

Political analyst and senior policy fellow at the European Council on Foreign Relations, Andrew Wilson, has cast doubt on Arseny Yatseniuk, currently Ukraine's third most popular candidate, ability to maintain his meteoritic rise following a decline in his ratings dropping from a high of 13% in August to 9% in October. ''"Yatseniuk must look to plan B"''<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/opinion/op_ed/51159 Yatseniuk loses fresh-face label, popularity after his financial backers exposed], [[Kyiv Post]] (22 October 2009)</ref>

On 6 November the nominations were closed. The same day a Viktor Yushchenko aide amidst concern over the [[2009 flu pandemic in Ukraine|recent flu outbreak]] which claimed 97 lives has proposed the cancellation of the January election until May 2010 which would extend the President's term of office a further six months.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/52092 Flu epidemic in Ukraine may require postponement of elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101113051117/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/52092/ |date=13 November 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (6 November 2009)</ref> The [[World Health Organization]] has stated that they expect a second and third wave of infections to occur in Spring (April to June)<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/52054 WHO experts forecast three waves of A/H1N1 flu in Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091108013152/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/52054/ |date=8 November 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (6 November 2009)</ref> bringing into further doubt Yushchenko's proposed cancellation. Under Ukraine's [[s:Constitution of Ukraine|Constitution]] the elections can be canceled if a [[State of Emergency]] is declared. Also on 6 November 2009, the Emergencies Ministry stated it saw no grounds to introduce a state of emergency in Ukraine due to the flu epidemic.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/52083/ Emergency ministry sees no grounds for state of emergency] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091108013223/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/52083/ |date=8 November 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (6 November 2009)</ref> On 9 November President Yushchenko said the same.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-345792.html Yushchenko: there are no grounds for state of emergency] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091112215315/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-345792.html |date=12 November 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (9 November 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-345757.html Ukraine president: no reason to delay vote over flu] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091111050159/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-345757.html |date=11 November 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (9 November 2009)</ref>

Serhy Lutsenko, the deputy head of the People's Self-Defense party expressed on 11 November 2009 concern that Viktor Yushchenko will support his past rival, Viktor Yanukovych, in a run-off election between Yanukovych and Tymoshenko.<ref>[http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2009/11/10/204057 Yushchenko to back Yanukovych in runoffs] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091119235852/http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2009/11/10/204057 |date=19 November 2009 }}, [[ZIK]] (11 November 2009)</ref>

On 3 December 2009, the Ukrainian National Council on Television and Radio Broadcasting complained that certain [[List of television stations in Ukraine|TV channels]] did not give equal conditions to all presidential candidates.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/54264/ National Council: Ukrainian TV and radio companies not giving equal conditions to presidential candidates] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110201024340/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/54264/ |date=1 February 2011 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (3 December 2009)</ref>

On 11 December 2009, the [[European People's Party]] EPP called on "Ukraine's democratic forces" to unite around the most democratic candidate who will win through to the presidential run-off. [[All-Ukrainian Union "Fatherland"]], the [[Our Ukraine People's Union]], and the [[People's Movement of Ukraine]] (Rukh) are the EPP's partners in Ukraine.<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/27360/ EPP calls on Ukraine's democratic forces to unite around most democratic candidate] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091213182737/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/27360 |date=13 December 2009 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (9 December 2009)</ref>

On 11 December 2009, candidate Viktor Yanukovych stated that his Party of Regions possesses information that "government representatives are currently "motivating" the chairmen of election commissions and seeking options for victory in every possible way" and called for his supporters go to the [[Maidan Nezalezhnosti]] in case of [[election fraud]].<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/27579/ Yanukovych vows to gather people on Maidan if election results are rigged] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212650/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/27579/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11 December 2009)</ref>

Bloc Yulia Tymoshenko proposes a referendum on the format of Constitutional reform - [[Parliamentary]] versus [[Presidential system|Presidential]] system of governance.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59573/ BYT proposes discussing form of government, constitutional amendments to referendum] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100218004701/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59573/ |date=18 February 2010 }} [[Kyiv Post]], 15 February 2010</ref>

===Issues===
The list of major issues raised in the campaign included
* The economy<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50996 Ukrainians blame Yuschenko (47%) and Tymoshenko (22%) for creating economic mess] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200312130335/https://www.kyivpost.com/article/content/ukraine-politics/ukrainians-blame-yuschenko-and-tymoshenko-for-crea-50996.html |date=12 March 2020 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (20 August 2009)</ref>
* Health
* Housing
* Ukraine's membership of [[NATO]] and [[CSTO]]<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53640/ Poll: over 40 percent of Ukrainians prefer Collective Security Treaty Organization, 12.5 percent favor NATO] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120606143750/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53640/ |date=6 June 2012 }} (26 November 2009)</ref>
* European integration
* [[Russia–Ukraine relations|Relations with Russia]]<ref>[http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/text/speeches/2009/08/11/0832_type207221_220745.shtml/ Relations between Russia and Ukraine: a New Era Must Begin] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090813224408/http://www.kremlin.ru/eng/text/speeches/2009/08/11/0832_type207221_220745.shtml/ |date=2009-08-13 }}, [[Dmitry Anatolyevich Medvedev|Demitry Medvedev]] (11 August 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/47194 Yushchenko calling on Medvedev to intensify Russian-Ukrainian dialog], [[Kyiv Post]] (19 August 2009)</ref>
* Constitutional reform
* [[UEFA Euro 2012]] football tournament
* Status of the [[Russian language]]

According to the Director of the Penta Center for Political Studies Volodymyr Fesenko, there were only small differences in the election programs of the various candidates.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53400/ Experts: Presidential campaign characterized by poor programs of candidates] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110217051933/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53400/ |date=17 February 2011 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (23 November 2009)</ref>

==Opinion polls==
According to an article in ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' in November 2009, Yanukovych's popularity in the [[Donbas]] was fading and Donbas voters voted mainly for Yanukovych to keep Tymoshenko from power.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53727/ "Voting for Yanukovych, but unenthusiastically"], ''Kyiv Post'' (26 November 2009)</ref>

A poll released 15 December 2009 by the [[International Foundation for Electoral Systems]] has indicated that Viktor Yanukovych (31%) as the most likely to win the presidential election in a contest with Yulia Tymoshenko (19%).<ref name=ifes>{{cite press release|publisher=International Foundation for Electoral Systems|url=http://www.ifes.org/publication/9c648aca6bb32dc209a4384513da12d2/IFES_UkraineSurvey2009_PR.pdf|date=15 December 2009|access-date=16 December 2009|title=Archived copy|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110616183442/http://www.ifes.org/publication/9c648aca6bb32dc209a4384513da12d2/IFES_UkraineSurvey2009_PR.pdf|archive-date=16 June 2011|url-status=dead|df=dmy-all}}</ref> All other candidates were below 5% with Viktor Yushchenko on 3.5% with a negativity rating of 83%. The survey also indicated that Ukrainians are pessimistic about the socio-political situation in the country. Seventy-four percent believe Ukraine is on a path toward instability and more than nine in ten Ukrainians are dissatisfied with the economic (96%) and political situation (92%) in the country.

According to other recent opinion polls, the [[Party of Regions]] candidate [[Viktor Yanukovych]] (25.0% to 33.3%) was placed first among viable presidential candidates, with [[Prime Minister of Ukraine|Prime Minister]] [[Yulia Tymoshenko]] (15.5% to 18.4%) coming in second, and [[Yatsenyuk's Front for Change|Front for Change]] candidate [[Arseniy Yatsenyuk]] (6.7% to 14.5%) in third place. Incumbent President, [[Viktor Yushchenko]] (2.0% to 3.8%) following his decline in popularity with the Ukrainian public comes in at a distant sixth place behind leader of the [[Communist Party of Ukraine|Communist Party]] [[Petro Symonenko]] (3.4% to 4.5%) and [[Parliamentary speaker]] [[Volodymyr Lytvyn]] (1.4% to 5.8%).<ref>{{cite news|url=http://bd.fom.ru/report/map/ukrain/ukrain_eo/du090430|title=Yushchenko approval rating|work=[[FOM-Ukraine]]|access-date=22 May 2009|archive-date=27 July 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110727124630/http://bd.fom.ru/report/map/ukrain/ukrain_eo/du090430|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|author=Taras Kuzio|author-link=Taras Kuzio|url=https://www.kyivpost.com/article/opinion/op-ed/with-or-without-baloha-yushchenkos-unelectable-2-305121.html|title=With or without Baloha, Yushchenko's unelectable|publisher=[[Kyiv Post]]|date=24 October 2009|access-date=1 March 2016|archive-date=1 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160301090716/https://www.kyivpost.com/article/opinion/op-ed/with-or-without-baloha-yushchenkos-unelectable-2-305121.html|url-status=live}}</ref>

A survey conducted by U.S.-based International Foundation for Electoral Systems and financed by the United States Agency for International Development (21–29 November) lists Viktor Yushchenko as the highest negativity rating (83%) and Viktor Yanukovych with the most positive rating (42%)<ref name="KIISPoll">{{in lang|en}}[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/55401/ "Voters unhappy with choices, want jobs"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091219184443/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/55401/ |date=19 December 2009 }}, (21–29 November) [[International Foundation for Electoral Systems]]</ref>

An opinion poll conducted by [[FOM-Ukraine]] in September/October 2009 expected the turnout to be at least 85.1%.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50541 Survey: most Ukrainians ready to vote in presidential polls], [[Kyiv Post]] (12 October 2009)</ref> The poll carried out by the Oleksandr Yaremenko Institute for Social Research in December 2009 predicted (at least) a 70% turnout.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55800/ Pollster predicts 70% turnout for presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091225203524/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55800/ |date=25 December 2009 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (23 December 2009)</ref>

2 January 2010 was the beginning of the 15-day media blackout on reporting of election polls before the 17 January first-round election.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56318/|title=Public reporting of poll results is prohibited in final 15&nbsp;days before presidential election|access-date=2010-01-02|date=2 January 2010|work=[[Interfax-Ukraine]]|publisher=[[Kyiv Post]]|archive-date=17 January 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100117194343/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56318/|url-status=live}}</ref>

