==Conduct==
The election was widely recognized and endorsed as being fair and an accurate reflection of voters' intentions by all international agencies observing the election including the OSCE and [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe|PACE]].<ref name=hellolazy>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8505552.stm Ukraine's Tymoshenko bloc 'contesting election result'], [[BBC News]] (9 February 2010)</ref><ref>[http://assembly.coe.int/ASP/NewsManager/EMB_NewsManagerView.asp?ID=5267 Run-off confirms that Ukraine's presidential election meets most international commitments] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110617195215/http://assembly.coe.int/ASP/NewsManager/EMB_NewsManagerView.asp?ID=5267 |date=17 June 2011 }}, [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe]] (8 February 2010)</ref> According to all international organizations observing the election, allegations of electoral fraud in relation to the first-round ballot were unfounded; they declared that the conduct of the elections was within internationally recognized democratic standards and a testament to the will of the people of Ukraine.<ref>[http://euobserver.com/?aid=29431 EU endorses Ukraine election result] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110605033800/http://euobserver.com/?aid=29431 |date=5 June 2011 }}, [[euobserver]] (8 February 2010)</ref><ref>[https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2010/02/08/AR2010020803583.html International observers say Ukrainian election was free and fair] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201012114207/https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2010/02/08/AR2010020803583.html |date=12 October 2020 }}, ''[[The Washington Post]]'' (9 February 2010)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59077/ European Parliament president greets Ukraine on conducting free and fair presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100211014322/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59077/ |date=11 February 2010 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (9 February 2010)</ref>

A December 2009 poll found that 82 percent of Ukrainians expected vote rigging, those fears were shared by some election observers, both international and domestic. The latter also fearing the lack of an independent exit poll; which they see as essential to deterring vote fraud.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/54838/ Election watchers worried by lack of independent exit poll; survey essential to deterring vote fraud], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (11 December 2009)</ref>

Yulia Tymoshenko, Ukraine's Prime-minister and one of the main candidates who sought election in the poll stated that: "We will not challenge any election returns to avoid tremors, which may bring about instability in this country. If the people elect their president, and this is not Yulia Tymoshenko, I will take this choice easy, for sure"<ref>[http://forua.wordpress.com/2009/09/25/tymoshenko-will-not-protest-presidential-election-outcome/ Tymoshenko will not protest presidential election outcome] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110718095952/http://forua.wordpress.com/2009/09/25/tymoshenko-will-not-protest-presidential-election-outcome/|date=18 July 2011}}, [[ForUm]] (25 September 2009)</ref>

Former president [[Leonid Kuchma]] also excluded the possibility of a third-round ballot. According to Kuchma, "during the election campaign in 2004 the decision about holding the third round was political and it will not be repeated. The 2004 decision was an exclusion from a rule".<ref>[http://nrcu.gov.ua/index.php?id=148&listid=100472 Ex-president excludes third round of presidential elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110220213614/http://nrcu.gov.ua/index.php?id=148&listid=100472|date=2011-02-20}}, [[National Radio Ukraine]] (25 September 2009)</ref>

Viktor Baloha, former presidential secretary under Viktor Yushchenko stated:

:"Alarming declarations about the likely vote rigging directly point to organizational weaknesses of some candidates as the law allows for reliable barriers against any electoral fraud. For instance, any presidential candidate can send his two representatives to sit on local and regional electoral commissions, appoint observers to keep an eye on voting and counting of ballots. Proxies of candidates who have wide authority can also supervise the course of the voting". "There are more than enough supervisory tools. Other effective barriers to electoral fraud are the Central Election Commission [whose members are appointed by major parliamentary parties on a quota principle] and numerous international observers. Mass media and NGOs, notably, the Committee of Voters of Ukraine, will also be effective in helping to curb fraud. Of great importance for establishing the final tally are also exit polls run by respected polling companies.they will all be used during the campaign." adding that "All the more so that there are 18 presidential candidates, some having considerable weight. That is why any declarations about the likely fraud are just attempts to justify a defeat of those who make them. Note that those candidates who are selling themselves as strong-willed and tough are most given to such declarations. In fact, such declarations expose them as would-be losers and outsiders"<ref>[http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2009/11/24/205864 Wolf-crying about likely vote rigging presidential candidates try to justify their future defeat], [[ZIK]] (24 November 2009)</ref>

Candidates [[Viktor Yanukovych]] and [[Yulia Tymoshenko]] both accused the other of intending to use [[vote rigging]] to win the election during the [[election campaign]].<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55333/ Yanukovych sure Tymoshenko will try to rig results of presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609120607/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55333/ |date=9 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (17 December 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55336/ Tymoshenko says she will prevent Yanukovych from rigging presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609120622/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55336/ |date=9 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (17 December 2009)</ref> Early January 2010 [[Ukrainian president]] [[Viktor Yushchenko]] warned that there is a real threat of "[[Administrative resource|administrative pressure]]" being applied during the counting of votes at the presidential election. Viktor Yushchenko without providing any details has alleged that the highest threat of falsification in the first round will be applied by [[Yulia Tymoshenko Bloc]]; "because candidate Viktor Yanukovych will enter the second round in any case".<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/29250/ Yuschenko warns of significant threat of administrative pressure during counting of votes] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120515212705/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/29250/ |date=15 May 2012 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (5 January 2010)</ref>

Allegations were made that Viktor Yushchenko had made a deal with Viktor Yanukovych in order to secure a number of political positions for members of his team in exchange for supporting Viktor Yanukovych's campaign <ref>{{Deprecated archive|url=https://archive.today/20130418224857/http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2010/01/08/211289|title=President's office ex-official blows Yushchenko-Yanukovych secret deal}}, [[ZIK]] (8 January 2010)</ref> Concern has been expressed that Viktor Yushchenko had tried to prevent news of the deal from being published by declaring it a State Secret.<ref>[http://www.jamestown.org/single/?no_cache=1&tx_ttnews[tt_news&#93;=35871&tx_ttnews {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160902144658/http://www.jamestown.org/single/?no_cache=1 |date=2016-09-02 }}[backPid&#93;=7&cHash=e9627f75db Yushchenko and Yanukovych Forge an Electoral Alliance – [[Taras Kuzio]], [[Jamestown Foundation]] (8 January 2010)</ref>

A joint poll conducted by Democratic Initiatives and Ukrainian Sociology Service of January 2010 showed that less than 5% of the polled believed that the presidential election would be fair with 41.4% of respondents that believed that the election results could be manipulated and 15.7% being certain that the entire vote would be rigged.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56820/ Poll: Less than 5% Ukrainians believe presidential election will be fair] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609120630/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56820/ |date=9 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (12 January 2010)</ref> According to the same poll, 5.8% of those polled stated they were ready to sell their votes if the sum suited them and 1.9% of the respondents were ready to sell their votes for any presidential candidates and for any funds.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56815/ Poll: Most Ukrainians not planning to sell their votes in presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609120651/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56815/ |date=9 June 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (12 January 2010)</ref>

A voter casting more than one ballot, or selling their vote, could have faced as much as two years jail time.<ref>[http://www.earthtimes.org/articles/show/303574,ukraine-presidential-candidates-trade-warnings-promises—summary.html Ukraine presidential candidates trade warnings, promises – Summary] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160304071931/http://www.earthtimes.org/articles/show/303574,ukraine-presidential-candidates-trade-warnings-promises%E2%80%94summary.html |date=4 March 2016 }}, Earth Times (13 January 2010)</ref>

The Ukrainian Central Election Commission and international observers found no evidence of significant electoral fraud and said that the voting and counting was fair.<ref name=BBCresults>{{Cite web |url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8506491.stm |title=Ukraine instability fears as Tymoshenko plans next move |date=9 February 2010 |access-date=10 February 2010 |archive-date=16 January 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116221151/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/8506491.stm |url-status=live }}</ref>

===International observers===
The [[Ministry of Foreign Affairs (Ukraine)|Ukrainian Foreign Ministry]] expected (in November 2009) that some 600 international observers would be monitoring the elections.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53402/ PACE, OSCE election observers are arriving in Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110217051854/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53402/ |date=17 February 2011 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (23 November 2009)</ref> The [[Organization for Security and Cooperation in Europe]] (OSCE) will send around 60 long-term and 600 short-term observers to Ukraine to monitor the presidential elections, Ukraine had submitted an invitation to the OSCE to monitor the elections.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/nation/50613 Over 600 OSCE observers to monitor presidential elections in Ukraine], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (13 October 2009)</ref>
This electoral observation mission is headed by [[Portuguese people|Portuguese]] politician [[João Soares (politician)|João Soares]], President of the [[OSCE Parliamentary Assembly]]. The OSCE/ODIHR long term observation mission was officially opened on 26 November 2009.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/53662/ OSCE/ODIHR officially opens election observation mission for presidential election in Ukraine] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120523182503/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/53662/ |date=23 May 2012 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (26 November 2009)</ref> On 12 January 2009, the OSCE where not satisfied with the level of funding for salaries and transport services.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56827/ OSCE observer: Ukrainian election lacks funding for salaries, transport services] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110124142010/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56827/ |date=24 January 2011 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (12 January 2010)</ref>

The [[European Union]] member-states will send over 700 observers to monitor the elections.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/53040/ European Union to send over 700 observers to monitor Ukraine's presidential elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101113045234/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/53040/ |date=13 November 2010 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (17 November 2009)</ref> The Canada Ukraine Foundation<ref>[http://cufoundation.ca/eom2010/ Election Observer Mission 2010] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100117085154/http://www.cufoundation.ca/EOM2010 |date=2010-01-17 }}, Canada Ukraine Foundation</ref> (a [[Canada|Canadian]] [[NGO]]<ref>[http://www.cufoundation.ca/about About us] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080906112345/http://www.cufoundation.ca/about |date=2008-09-06 }}, Canada Ukraine Foundation</ref>) and the [[Parliamentary Assembly of the Council of Europe]] (PACE) will also send observers.<ref>[http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-348265.html PACE delegation to pay visit to Ukraine on November 24–26] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091125174542/http://www.unian.net/eng/news/news-348265.html |date=25 November 2009 }}, [[UNIAN]] (23 November 2009)</ref> The PACE delegation is led by [[Hungary|Hungarian]] politician [[Mátyás Eörsi]].<ref name=Eorsi>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/53713/ European lawmakers' hopes low for Ukraine vote], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (26 November 2009)</ref> Late November the PACE delegation was sceptical the elections would meet the organization's standards.<ref name=Eorsi/> On 8 December 2009, [[Renate Wohlwend]], co-rapporteur of PACE stated that PACE might continue to monitor [[Ukrainian politics]] after the country's presidential election.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/54575/ PACE may keep monitoring Ukraine after presidential poll], [[Kyiv Post]] (8 December 2009)</ref> Wohlwend had also called on the Ukrainian parliament to amend a law on the presidential elections as soon as possible. Wohlwend expressed concern over the inclusion of a provision in Ukraine's electoral legislation giving the election commission the right to amend the electoral rolls on the day of the ballot. She expressed concern this could allow the [[Electoral fraud|rigging of the election results]].<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/54627/ PACE rapporteur calling on Ukraine's parliament to amend law on presidential elections], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (9 December 2009)</ref>

The [[Poland|Polish]] European Center of Geopolitical Analysis did send 20 observers to monitor [[Racism and discrimination in Ukraine|signs of xenophobia]] during the presidential election campaign.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/54385/ Polish observers to arrive in Ukraine to monitor signs of xenophobia during election campaign], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (4 December 2009)</ref>

On 9 December 2009, candidate [[Viktor Yanukovych]] at a meeting with an OSCE election observation mission stated that he is afraid Prime Minister [[Yulia Tymoshenko]] might rig the presidential election.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/54679/ Yanukovych claims to OSCE that Ukrainian government intends to rig presidential election], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (9 December 2009)</ref>

A total of 450 official observers from the European Network of Election Monitoring Organizations (ENEMO) will monitor the elections.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55130/ 450 observers from ENEMO international mission to monitor Ukrainian elections], [[Kyiv Post]] (15 December 2009)</ref>

[[Paweł Kowal]] lead the delegation of the [[European Parliament]]'s observers; this delegation included ten people, who cooperated closely with the delegations of observers from the [[OSCE Parliamentary Assembly]], the Council of Europe, the [[NATO Parliamentary Assembly]], and the OSCE [[Office for Democratic Institutions and Human Rights]] (ODIHR).<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55225/ Kowal to head delegation of European Parliament's observers for elections] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110605123626/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/55225/ |date=5 June 2011 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (16 December 2009)</ref>

A total of 3,149 international observers did monitor the 17 January presidential election in Ukraine.<ref>[http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/29514/ Central Election Commission fails to register over 2,000 official observers from Georgia] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100121061407/http://www.interfax.com.ua/eng/main/29514 |date=21 January 2010 }}, [[Interfax-Ukraine]] (11, January 2010)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56708/ Over 3,000 international observers registered for Ukrainian presidential election] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110124141331/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/56708/ |date=24 January 2011 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (11, January 2010)</ref>

On 18 January 2010, the OSCE announced it would send same number of observers to monitor Ukraine's second round of the election as in the first round.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57410/ OSCE to send same number of observers to monitor Ukraine's runoff], ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (18 January 2010)</ref> At the same time it called for bringing Ukraine's election laws in line with international norms<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57399/ OSCE observers: Ukraine's election laws should be brought in line with international norms], [[Kyiv Post]] (18 January 2010)</ref> but nevertheless it endorsed the first round of the Ukrainian presidential poll, saying it was of "high quality" and demonstrated "significant progress".<ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8466389.stm Ukraine election: And then there were two], [[BBC News]] (18 January 2010)</ref>

After the second round of the election international observers and the OSCE called the election transparent and honest.<ref name="nocomment"/>

According to Serhiy Paskhalov, the head of presidential candidate Yulia Tymoshenko's main campaign office in [[Dnipropetrovsk]], international observers were physically unable to register mass irregularities in the second round of the presidential election. According to Paskhalov six foreign observers had monitored the run-off presidential election at 469 polling stations in six electoral districts in Dnipropetrovsk region.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59297/ BYT: International observers were physically unable to record mass irregularities in run-off vote] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120612031534/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59297/ |date=12 June 2012 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (11 February 2009)</ref>

