The first round of the election was held on 17 January 2010. Voter turnout was approximately 67 percent, compared to 75 percent at [[2004 Ukrainian presidential election|the 2004 presidential election]]. Early-vote returns from the first round of the election held on 17 January showed Yanukovych in first place with 36% of the vote.<ref name="wsj.com">{{cite news|title=Ukrainian Presidential Election Set for Runoff|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704541004575010390901914042?mod=WSJ_latestheadlines|work=The Wall Street Journal|date=18 January 2010|access-date=2010-01-18|first=James|last=Marson|archive-date=16 January 2023|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230116220529/https://www.wsj.com/articles/SB10001424052748704541004575010390901914042?mod=WSJ_latestheadlines|url-status=live}}</ref> He faced a 7 February 2010 runoff against Tymoshenko, who finished second (with 25% of the vote). Incumbent president [[Viktor Yushchenko]] was defeated having received only 6% of the vote, finishing in fifth place behind Serhiy Tihipko and Arseniy Yatsenyuk who had each respectively received 13% and 7% of the vote.<ref>{{in lang|uk}} [http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp320pt00_t001f01=700pt001f01=700pplace=1.html Central Election Commission Candidate Results] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100121012542/http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp320pt00_t001f01=700pt001f01=700pplace=1.html |date=2010-01-21 }}, [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]] (19 January 2010)</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57325/ TABLE-Ukraine's presidential election results] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100121145235/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/57325 |date=21 January 2010 }}, ''[[Kyiv Post]]'' (18 January 2010)</ref><ref>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8464816.stm Ukraine's Orange leader Yushchenko loses election], [[BBC News]] (18 January 2010)</ref>

Peter Simonenko, Volodymyr Lytvyn, Oleh Tyahnybok and Anatoliy Hrytsenko all scored between 4 and 1% of the votes. The remaining nine candidates for the presidency gained less than 1% of the votes.<ref>{{in lang|uk}} [http://gazeta.ua/index.php?id=324402 ЦВК оприлюднила офіційні результати 1-го туру виборів] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021195659/http://gazeta.ua/index.php?id=324402 |date=21 October 2014 }}, [[Gazeta.ua]] (25 January 2010)</ref>

Analysts predicted that both candidates had a chance to win the second round.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/57292/ Analysts say exit poll results favor Tymoshenko win on Feb. 7], ''Kyiv Post'' (18 January 2010)</ref> Yanukovych refused to hold debates with his opponent before the second round of voting, saying Tymoshenko should either take responsibility for every word as prime minister, or go to the kitchen.<ref>http://finchannel.com/Main_News/Ukraine/56415_Yanukovych_Unwilling_To_Debate_Tymoshenko_At_Runoff_Election_/ {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190404192737/https://finchannel.com/Main_News/Ukraine/56415_Yanukovych_Unwilling_To_Debate_Tymoshenko_At_Runoff_Election_/ |date=4 April 2019 }}, [[The Financial]] (1 February 2010)</ref> After all ballots were counted, the Ukrainian Central Election Commission declared that Yanukovych won the election with 48.95% of the vote compared with 45.47% for Tymoshenko.<ref name=2010results>{{in lang|uk}}[http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp300pt001f01=701.html Regular elections of the President of Ukraine 17/01/2010] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100121015545/http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp300pt001f01%3D701.html |date=2010-01-21 }}, [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]]</ref> [[Yulia Tymoshenko Bloc]] members immediately claimed that there was systematic and large-scale vote rigging in this run-off.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59198/ Turchynov: vote rigging in favor of Yanukovych was systematic, large-scale] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120609121309/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59198/ |date=9 June 2012 }}, ''Kyiv Post'' (10 February 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/6za4s343 Nataliya Korolevska: victory will be ours] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140815142406/http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/6za4s343 |date=2014-08-15 }}, Yulia Tymoshenko official website (8 February 2009)</ref><ref>[http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/37u69796 Andriy Shevchenko: whole gamut of fraud in Donbas] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140815142413/http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/37u69796 |date=2014-08-15 }}, Yulia Tymoshenko official website (8 February 2009)</ref><ref name=BBCprotest>[https://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/world/europe/8508276.stm Ukraine election: Yanukovych urges Tymoshenko to quit], [[BBC News]] (10 February 2009)</ref>

The second round of voting between Viktor Yanukovych and Yulia Tymoshenko took place on 7 February 2010. [[Exit poll]]s indicated that Yanukovych had been elected, with the National Election Poll placing him first at 49% of the vote to Tymoshenko's 46%.<ref>{{cite web |url=http://english.ruvr.ru/2010/02/08/4242868.html |title=Ukrainian elections: anything possible |access-date=2010-11-08 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100220145436/http://english.ruvr.ru/2010/02/08/4242868.html |archive-date=2010-02-20 |url-status=dead }}</ref> With 100% of the ballots counted, the tally was 12,481,268 votes for Yanukovych and 11,593,340 for Tymoshenko.<ref name=results2ndround>{{cite web |url=http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp300pt001f01=701.html |access-date=2010-02-10 |title=Archived copy |archive-date=21 January 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100121015545/http://www.cvk.gov.ua/vp2010/wp300pt001f01%3D701.html |url-status=live }}</ref> There were 1.19% invalid votes and 4.36% of voters chose to vote "Against all" (candidates).<ref name=results2ndround/> In [[Kyiv]], the number of voter choosing "Against all" was close to 8%.<ref>[https://ottawacitizen.com/business/Vote+spoils+Ukraine+NATO+hopes/2543742/story.html Vote spoils Ukraine's EU, NATO hopes]{{Dead link|date=January 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}, [[Ottawa Citizen]] (10 February 2010)</ref>

