==Analysis==
Voting analysis showed that during the election creases started to emerge across the traditional geographical voters patterns. Tymoshenko made inroads in Yanukovych's traditional [[East Ukraine|east]] and [[south Ukraine]] base of support, whereas Yanukovych did the same in Tymoshenko's traditional west and [[central Ukraine]] base of support.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59340/ Election winner lacks strong voter mandate] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100217083456/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59340/ |date=17 February 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (11 February 2010)</ref> More women voted for Yanukovych than for Tymoshenko.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/opinion/editorial/detail/62532/ Neanderthal power] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100329145027/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/opinion/editorial/detail/62532 |date=29 March 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (25 March 2010)</ref>

==Reactions==
A few days after the election, Yanukovich received congratulations from the leaders of [[Armenia]], [[Austria]], [[Azerbaijan]], [[Belarus]], [[Bulgaria]], [[China]], [[Egypt]], [[Estonia]], [[Finland]], [[France]], [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]], [[Germany]], [[Greece]], [[Hungary]], [[Israel]], [[Italy]], [[Kazakhstan]], [[Latvia]], [[Libya]], [[Lithuania]], [[Moldova]], the [[Netherlands]], [[Paraguay]], [[Poland]], [[Portugal]], [[Russia]], [[Slovakia]], [[Slovenia]], [[Spain]], [[Sweden]], [[Tajikistan]], [[Turkey]], the [[United Kingdom]], the [[United States]], [[Uzbekistan]], [[NATO]] and the [[European Union]].<ref>{{cite web|url = http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59306/|title = Obama praises level of democratic development in Ukraine - Feb. 11, 2010|date = 11 February 2010|access-date = 12 February 2010|archive-date = 31 January 2011|archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110131222717/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59306/|url-status = live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.partyofregions.org.ua/eng/pr-east-west/4b72cc95d7f26/ |title=Сайт www.partyofregions.org.ua не настроен на сервере |publisher=Partyofregions.org.ua |date= |access-date=2022-03-08 |archive-date=13 February 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100213173402/http://www.partyofregions.org.ua/eng/pr-east-west/4b72cc95d7f26/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59372/ NATO, EU follow U.S., welcome Yanukovych] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110201054755/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59372/ |date=1 February 2011 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (12 February 2010)</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://unian.net/rus/news/news-362520.html |title=Януковича поздравили еще 5 президентов |date=12 February 2010 |access-date=15 February 2010 |archive-date=13 August 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110813141844/http://www.unian.net/rus/news/news-362520.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{cite web |url=http://zadonbass.org/news/all/message_7912 |title=Продолжается поток поздравлений в адрес Януковича |access-date=2010-02-15 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110614082346/http://zadonbass.org/news/all/message_7912 |archive-date=2011-06-14 |url-status=dead }}</ref> Still, Tymoshenko refused to concede defeat, and [[BYuT|Tymoshenko's party]] promised to challenge the result.<ref name=nocomment>[https://www.rferl.org/a/Ukraines_Tymoshenko_Slams_Rival_No_Comment_On_Election_Result/1954993.html Ukraine's Tymoshenko Slams Rival, No Comment On Election Result], [[Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty]] (11 February 2010)</ref> On 17 February 2010, the Administrative Court of Ukraine, suspended the results of the election on Yulia Tymoshenko's appeal. The court suspended the [[Central Election Commission (Ukraine)|Central Election Commission of Ukraine]] ruling that announced that Viktor Yanukovych won the election.<ref name="APappeal1"/><ref name="BBCappeal2"/> Tymoshenko withdrew her appeal on 20 February 2010, after the Higher Administrative Court in Kyiv rejected her petition to scrutinize documents from election districts in [[Crimea]] and also to question election and law-enforcement officials.<ref name="NYTPMD"/> The same day (20 February) Tymoshenko announced that she will not challenge the results of the second round of the presidential election in the [[Supreme Court of Ukraine]] since she believed there were no legal provisions for such an appeal,<ref name="tymoshenko.ua">[http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/vu8az6s6 Yulia Tymoshenko will not challenge election results in Supreme Court] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20151018204137/http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/vu8az6s6 |date=2015-10-18 }}, Official website of Yulia Tymoshenko (20 February 2009)</ref> although Tymoshenko also stated "an honest court will assess that Yanukovych wasn't elected President of Ukraine, and that the will of the people had been rigged".<ref name="dishonest"/>

==Aftermath==
[[BYuT|Tymoshenko's party]] said that it would challenge the result in 1,000 of the country's 30,000 ballot boxes (as many as 900,000 ballots – enough to make a difference in the outcome<ref name=hercase/>), claiming that the counting process was unfair.<ref name=BBCresults/> Violations cited by Tymoshenko's camp included home voting and the busing of voters to polling stations,<ref name=hercase>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59339/ Tymoshenko cries foul: What's her case?] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100214103833/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59339 |date=14 February 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (10 February 2010)</ref> which was explicitly permitted by law. [[Party of Regions|Yanukovich's party]] activists rallied outside the buildings of the [[Higher Administrative Court of Ukraine]] and the Kyiv Administrative Court of Appeals the days after the second round of the election.<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/city/detail/59203/ Regions Party puts activists patrol near two courts in Kyiv] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100212102933/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/city/detail/59203/ |date=12 February 2010 }}, [[Kyiv Post]] (10 February 2010)</ref>

On 10 February 2010, Yanukovych called on Tymoshenko to abandon her protests and resign as prime minister.<ref name=BBCprotest/> On 9 February 2010, Yanukovych had stated that [[Borys Kolesnykov]] was his preferred next [[Prime Minister of Ukraine]]. According to him [[Next Ukrainian parliamentary election|pre-term parliamentary elections]] will be imminent if the [[Ukrainian parliament]] would not work effectively. Yanukovych also stated that, as the largest faction in the parliament at the time, his party was entitled to nominate the premier.<ref>[http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2010/02/09/216009 "Yanukovych opts for Borys Kolesnykov to become premier"] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141021233500/http://zik.com.ua/en/news/2010/02/09/216009 |date=21 October 2014 }}, Z I K (9 February 2010)</ref> On 15 February, Yanukovych stated "I do not rule out the candidature of [[Tigipko]] (as next Prime Minister). Tigipko is on the list which, in my opinion, will be discussed next week in [[Verkhovna Rada|parliament]]".<ref>[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59550/ Yanukovych does not rule out Tigipko as prime minister] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120612031018/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/nation/detail/59550/ |date=12 June 2012 }}, ''Kyiv Post'' (15 February 2010)</ref>

On 16 February 2010 [[Ukraine's parliament]] had fixed 25 February 2010 for the inauguration of Yanukovych as president.<ref name="kyivpost.com">[http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59629/ Update: Ukraine's Yanukovych to be sworn in on Feb. 25] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120612031056/http://www.kyivpost.com/news/politics/detail/59629/ |date=12 June 2012 }}, ''Kyiv Post'' (16 February 2010)</ref> On 17 February 2010, "the Higher Administrative Court of Ukraine", suspended the results of the election on Yulia Tymoshenko's appeal.<ref name="APappeal1">[{{cite news|url=https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5hYHMIGxCNY8KpokmVs53ufhC8qIQD9DTT0H00|title=Ukrainian election results suspended on appeal|agency=[[Associated Press]]|date=17 February 2010|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100221065939/https://www.google.com/hostednews/ap/article/ALeqM5hYHMIGxCNY8KpokmVs53ufhC8qIQD9DTT0H00|archive-date=21 February 2010}}</ref><ref name="BBCappeal2">{{cite news|url=https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8519922.stm|title=Ukrainian election result suspended after PM's appeal|work=[[BBC News]]|date=17 February 2010|access-date=18 January 2016|archive-date=28 February 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170228052622/http://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/europe/8519922.stm|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 20 February 2010, Tymoshenko withdrew her appeal after "the Higher Administrative Court of Ukraine" rejected her petition to scrutinize documents:<ref name="tymoshenko.ua"/><br />
— about 300,000 voters who voted but were not in the "Register of Voters of Ukraine";<br />
— about 1.3 million voters who "without right" voted in their homes;<br />
— about falsification in the election in the eastern regions (Donetsk, Luhansk, Kharkiv region, Crimea, etc.) — fixed by law-enforcement officials.<ref name=NYTPMD/><br />
Tymoshenko stated, "I and my political party will never recognize Yanukovych as the legitimately elected president of Ukraine", and "an honest court will assess that Yanukovych was not elected President of Ukraine, and that the will of the people had been rigged".<ref name="dishonest">{{cite web|url=http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/uv4w2uwz|title=Yulia Tymoshenko: sooner or later an honest court will assess the fraudulent 2010 elections|work=Yulia Tymoshenko Official website|date=20 February 2010|url-status=dead|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100225000534/http://www.tymoshenko.ua/en/article/uv4w2uwz|archive-date=25 February 2010}}</ref>

